⚡ REENTRY PHASE — CONTROLLED DESCENT

Duration: 25 seconds
Aspect: 9:16 cinematic
FPS: 48
Style: Hyper-real orbital survival sequence
Genre: Atmospheric reentry / human vs elemental force
Tone: Controlled tension → violent compression → restrained survival

RULE ZERO

❌ No slow motion
❌ No stylized camera shake
❌ No decorative explosions
❌ No magical physics
❌ No dramatic orchestral swell at the end

Every movement must be caused by:
Thruster output
Rotational inertia
Atmospheric drag
Structural vibration

PHYSICS LAW
Zero gravity motion must conserve angular momentum.
Thruster bursts must produce visible reaction plumes.
Plasma bloom must begin subtly at high altitude, intensify with density.
Suit does NOT burn like a meteor — plasma trails flow around it.
Sound in vacuum = internal breathing + suit vibrations only.
Atmospheric sound fades in gradually as pressure increases.
No fire in vacuum.
No external explosion shockwaves in space.

CAMERA LAW
Primary: FPV-adjacent trailing perspective
Secondary: Tight orbital tracking during descent
Camera motion must always be motivated by:

Spin
Turbulence
G-force
Structural instability
No floating drone shots.

AUDIO LAW
Sound design drives emotion before visuals escalate.
Phase 1 — Vacuum Silence (0–5s)
Controlled breathing
Subtle suit servo whine
Radio static low under mix
Phase 2 — Drag Introduction (5–10s)
Low sub-bass pressure swell (felt before heard)
Micro crackling at suit surface
Breathing slightly faster
Phase 3 — Plasma Envelope (10–18s)
Roaring plasma friction
Metallic resonance through suit frame
Audio distortion under peak heat
Heartbeat faint but present

Phase 4 — Atmospheric Transition (18–25s)
Wind shear
Structural rattle
Internal cabin alarms muted, not theatrical
Final exhale in silence
No triumphant music.
If BGM is used: minimal industrial pulse ~110 BPM low in mix.

FULL TIMELINE
Scene 1 [0.0–0.8s]

Astronaut drifting above Earth.
Subtle rotational drift clockwise.
Sun glare streaks across visor.

HUD flicker appears faintly.

Breathing steady.

Scene 2 [0.8–1.8s]

Thruster burst left shoulder.
Visible cold gas plume.
Rotation increases unintentionally.
Camera rotates with astronaut — no external cut.
Breathing slightly sharper.

Scene 3 [1.8–3.2s]
HUD text (diegetic, inside visor):
TRAJECTORY LOCKED — AUTO DESCENT
Earth curvature dominates frame.
Horizon tilts aggressively.
Sub-bass pressure begins.

Scene 4 [3.2–5.0s]
Multiple short corrective bursts.
Gas jets visible, dissipating into vacuum.
Angular momentum slows but doesn’t stop.
Radio static intensifies briefly.

Scene 5 [5.0–7.5s]
Atmosphere boundary visible ahead.
Thin blue line glows.
Tiny micro-particles streak past suit.
Very faint plasma whisper at glove edges.
Breathing controlled but heavy.

Scene 6 [7.5–10.5s]
First real plasma bloom.
Orange glow licks across helmet edges.
Camera vibration begins — turbulence onset.
Sub-bass grows stronger.
No screaming.

Scene 7 [10.5–14.5s]
Full plasma envelope.
Flames stream backward due to drag vector.
Camera tumbles slightly from aerodynamic instability.
Metallic resonance inside suit.
Heartbeat audible.

Scene 8 [14.5–18.0s]
Peak heat.
Visual distortion from atmospheric compression.
Plasma shifts from orange to white-hot streaks.
Audio momentarily compresses — slight distortion.
White flash at exit of peak density.

Scene 9 [18.0–21.5s]
Plasma rapidly dissipates.
Wind shear replaces roar.
Suit stabilizes.
Rotation dampens naturally due to drag.
Breathing ragged but steady.

Scene 10 [21.5–24.0s]
Interior cut — small emergency capsule.
Astronaut slams into seat harness under G-force.
Cabin shaking realistically.
No heroic pose.

Scene 11 [24.0–25.0s]
Silence.
Helmet visor lifts slowly.
Heavy exhale.
Cut to black before relief.

VISUAL DETAILS
Ultra-detailed EVA suit wear and scuffing
Realistic atmospheric light scattering
Volumetric glow around plasma trails
Lens breathing under heat distortion
No exaggerated Hollywood fireball tail
Slight chromatic aberration at peak heat

Color grade progression:
Cold blue space → Gold plasma → White compression → Muted interior steel